    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">COPD, GOLD 3, group B (J44.9)</content>. Slightly better on dual bronchodilator therapy: CAT 19, down from 22, with less reliever use, but still mMRC grade 3. 6-minute walk 270 m with a lowest saturation of 89 percent, and resting saturation 93 percent, so he meets no criterion for long-term or exertional oxygen. Blood eosinophils 0.18 x10³/µL and no exacerbations, so an inhaled corticosteroid would add risk without benefit. The pulmonologist agrees with the GOLD grade and group and with the current inhaler. [S]<list><item>Continue tiotropium-olodaterol two inhalations each morning and albuterol as needed.</item><item>Pulmonary rehabilitation starts next week: 24 sessions over eight weeks, with a repeat 6-minute walk at the end.</item><item>Written COPD action plan reviewed.</item><item>PCV20 was planned today but was out of stock; give at the next visit.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Survey</td><td>COPD Assessment Test (CAT)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>19, down from 22 at intake; a change of 2 or more is meaningful</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>mMRC dyspnea grade</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Grade 3, unchanged</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Exacerbation history and GOLD group</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No moderate or severe exacerbation in the past 12 months; group B</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Pulse oximetry</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>93 percent at rest on room air</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Smoking status and cessation support</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Former smoker; no tobacco for about 30 years</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>BMI and nutritional status</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>BMI 23.4; weight stable</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Inhaler technique and adherence</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Correct technique; no missed doses</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>6-minute walk test</td><td>At pulmonary rehabilitation entry and exit, and for oxygen assessment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>270 m at rehabilitation entry; lowest saturation 89 percent</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Long-term oxygen assessment</td><td>As indicated: resting saturation 88 percent or less, confirmed twice at least three weeks apart</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Not met: 93 percent at rest and 89 percent at the lowest on exertion; no oxygen prescribed</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Arterial blood gas</td><td>As indicated: resting saturation 92 percent or less, oxygen qualification, or suspected hypercapnia</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Resting saturation 93 percent; no sign of hypercapnia</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Spirometry</td><td>At diagnosis, then at least yearly and with significant clinical change</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Diagnostic spirometry at intake: post-bronchodilator FEV1/FVC 0.43, FEV1 46 percent predicted; next in one year</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Alpha-1 antitrypsin</td><td>Once, at diagnosis</td><td>Up to date</td><td>138 mg/dL at intake, normal; not repeated</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Blood eosinophil count</td><td>At baseline and when an inhaled corticosteroid is considered</td><td>Up to date</td><td>0.18 x10³/µL at intake; no inhaled corticosteroid while there are no exacerbations</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>ECG and cardiovascular risk assessment</td><td>At baseline and after exacerbations</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Intake ECG in sinus rhythm with no abnormality; blood pressure normal</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Mood and comorbidity screening</td><td>At diagnosis and yearly</td><td>Up to date</td><td>PHQ-9 3 and GAD-2 1 at intake; no reflux, sleep, or nutrition concern</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Pulmonology consultation</td><td>When the diagnosis, severity, or treatment needs specialist review</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen today; GOLD 3, group B confirmed; current inhaler continued</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Pulmonary rehabilitation</td><td>Stable mMRC 2 or more or CAT 10 or more; within three weeks of any hospital stay for an exacerbation</td><td>Referred</td><td>Program begins next week</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Vaccination status</td><td>Reviewed yearly</td><td>Deferred</td><td>High-dose influenza vaccine this past autumn, zoster series complete, Tdap within ten years. PCV20 was planned today but the clinic was out of stock, so it was not given; to be given at the next visit</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Low-dose chest CT</td><td>As indicated, or yearly lung cancer screening if eligible</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>No suspected complication; not eligible for screening, because he quit more than 15 years ago</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>CAT score at least 2 points lower by the three-month review. In progress; target 2026-05-12 [S]</item><item>6-minute walk distance at least 30 meters farther after pulmonary rehabilitation. In progress; target 2026-05-12 [S]</item><item>No moderate or severe exacerbation over the six months after starting maintenance therapy. In progress; target 2026-08-11 [S]</item><item>Correct soft mist inhaler technique and a daily maintenance dose. Outcome by 2026-03-10: Correct technique on demonstration and no missed doses by dose counter at four weeks [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Global Strategy for the Diagnosis, Management, and Prevention of Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease, 2025 report (2025); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease monograph (2026); Pulmonary Rehabilitation for Adults with Chronic Respiratory Disease: An Official American Thoracic Society Clinical Practice Guideline (2023); Home Oxygen Therapy for Adults with Chronic Lung Disease: An Official American Thoracic Society Clinical Practice Guideline (2020)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Pulmonary rehabilitation from next week. COPD review in two months, after rehabilitation. Next scheduled visit 2026-05-12: three-month COPD review after pulmonary rehabilitation. [S]</paragraph></text>

